¶ APPENDIX XVI.

ON @@R1@@
>>>
<<<
@@R1@@
===
BEATS OF COMBINATIONAL TONES, AND ON COMBINATIONAL TONES IN THE SIREN AND HARMONIUM.

(See pp. 199a and note ⁎, also 155c to 158a.)

Let \(a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h\) be whole numbers. Let \(an\) and \(bn + \delta\) be the pitch numbers of the primes of two @@R1@@
>>>
<<<
@@R1@@
===
compound tones sounded simultaneously, where \(\delta\) is supposed to be very small in comparison with \(n\), and \(a\) and \(b\) are the smallest whole numbers by which the ratio \(a : b\) can be expressed. The pitch numbers of any pair of partial tones of @@R1@@
>>>
<<<
@@R1@@
===
these two compound tones will be \[acn \text{ and } bdn + d\delta.\]

These will beat with each other \(d\delta\) times in a second, if ¶ \[ac = bd \text{ or } \frac{a}{b} = \frac{d}{c}.\]

And since the ratio \(\frac{a}{b}\) is expressed in its lowest terms, @@R1@@
>>>
<<<
@@R1@@
===
the smallest values of \(d\) and \(c\) are \[d = a \text{ and } c = b,\] and their other values are \[d = ha \text{ and } c = hb.\]

Now \(c\) and \(d\) represent the ordinal numbers of the partial tones which beat together. Hence the lowest partials of this @@R1@@
>>>
<<<
@@R1@@
===
kind will be the \(b\)th partial of the compound \(a n\), and the \(a\)th partial of the compound \(bn + \delta\). The resulting number of beats is \(a \delta\).

In the same way the \(2b\)th partial of the first compound, and the \(2a\)th of the ¶ second give@@R1@@
>>>
<<<
@@R1@@
===
 \(2a\delta\) beats, and so on.

The first differential combinational tone of the two partials \(acn\) and \(bdn + d\delta\) is \[\pm [(bd - ac)n + d\delta]\] where the + or − sign has to be taken so that the whole expression is positive.

Footnote: ⁎ [The @@R1@@
>>>
<<<
@@R1@@
===
interval is found as the ratio of the pitch number to the same increased by the number in the next column to it; thus for *C* it is 64 : 64 + 16 = 4 : 5, and for *g'* it is 384 : 384 + 60 = 96 : 111, and from these I have calculated the cents as in p. 701 of @@R1@@
>>>
<<<
@@R1@@
===
the 1st edition.
If \(N\) be the pitch number, and \(D\) = duration of residual sensations or the reciprocal of the numbers of vibrations producing a continuous sound, 16, 26, &c., as in the next preceding column, then Prof. Mayer finds— \[D = @@R1@@
>>>
<<<
@@R1@@
===
\left(\frac{53248}{N + 23} + 24\right) \cdot \frac{1}{10000} \text{ seconds.}\] —*Translator.*]

Footnote: † [The interval is found as the ratio of the pitch number to the same increased by the last mentioned number of beats, thus 64 : 64 + 6·4. Prof. Mayer @@R1@@
>>>
<<<
@@R1@@
===
draws attention to the fact that his beats were all tones of the same pitch, whereas the beats of imperfect consonances are tones of variable pitch.—*Translator.*]
